IB","gene_id":"ENSG00000111348","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":1.1776e-15,"nucleus_log2fc":0.317273,"nucleus_score":8.31987,"pct_ad":0.391736,"pct_control":0.33894,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.749152,"donor_log2fc":0.143317,"donor_lfcse":0.135324,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0158687,"degas_log2fc":-0.0330916,"degas_padj":0.453104}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000111348","entrez_id":["397"],"uniprot":["P52566"],"ncbi_summary":"Members of the Rho (or ARH) protein family (see MIM 165390) and other Ras-related small GTP-binding proteins (see MIM 179520) are involved in diverse cellular events, including cell signaling, proliferation, cytoskeletal organization, and secretion. The GTP-binding proteins are active only in the GTP-bound state. At least 3 classes of proteins tightly regulate cycling between the GTP-bound and GDP-bound states: GTPase-activating proteins (GAPs), guanine nucleotide-releasing factors (GRFs), and GDP-dissociation inhibitors (GDIs). The GDIs, including ARHGDIB, decrease the rate of GDP dissociation from Ras-like GTPases (summary by Scherle et al., 1993 [PubMed 8356058]).[supplied by OMIM, Dec 2010].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P52566","text":"Regulates the GDP/GTP exchange reaction of the Rho proteins by inhibiting the dissociation of GDP from them, and the subsequent binding of GTP to them (PubMed:7512369, PubMed:8356058). Regulates reorganization of the actin cytoskeleton mediated by Rho family members (PubMed:8262133)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}